---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="About & Edition History — tipitaka2500.github.io"
  description="History of tipitaka2500.github.io: from an early static HTML conversion to the verified 2020 OpenPāḷi edition built with Astro, featuring critical apparatus footnotes, cross-references, indices, and page concordances."
  currentBook="about"
  lang="en"
>
  <main class="index-page">
    <div class="index-hero">
      <h1>About This Website &amp; Edition History</h1>
      <p>
        How <strong>tipitaka2500.github.io</strong> evolved from an early static HTML conversion into
        a complete, deterministically verified digital reconstruction of the 2020 World Tipiṭaka
        Edition.
      </p>
    </div>

    <article class="index-card" style="padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      <h2 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--text-emphasis);">1. The World Tipiṭaka Edition</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The World Tipiṭaka Edition</strong> (<em>Mahāsaṅgīti Tipiṭaka Buddhavasse 2500</em>)
        represents the 40-volume Roman-script edition of the Pāḷi Tipiṭaka produced by the M.L.
        Maniratana Bunnag Dhamma Society Fund under the patronage of His Holiness Somdet Phra
        Ñāṇasaṁvara, the Supreme Patriarch of Thailand.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        Derived from the Chaṭṭhasaṅgīti (Sixth Buddhist Council) text convened in B.E. 2500 (1957),
        the Roman-script edition underwent four complete cycles of canonical Pāḷi recitation and
        computer-assisted proofreading to verify all <strong>2,708,706 Pāḷi words</strong>
        (<strong>20,606,104 Roman letters</strong>) across the 88 books of the Vinayapiṭaka,
        Suttantapiṭaka, and Abhidhammapiṭaka. For the complete historical timeline and linguistic
        innovations of the project (1893–2024), see{' '}
        <a href="/project.html"><strong>The World Tipiṭaka Project &amp; Its History →</strong></a>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="index-card" style="padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      <h2 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--text-emphasis);">2. Motivation: Why This Website Was Created</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Restoring a Lost Digital Heritage:</strong> When the original World Tipiṭaka website
        ceased operating and went offline, this monumental 40-volume Roman-script edition of the
        Great International Buddhist Council was in danger of disappearing from the open web. This
        site was originally founded to restore a permanent, free, fast, and accessible digital home for
        the World Tipiṭaka so that this historic resource would remain preserved and accessible to everyone.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Making the 2020 Edition Fully Functional:</strong> The <a href="https://openpali.sajjhaya.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open Pāḷi</a> website contains the latest digital version of the World Tipiṭaka Edition
        but remains incomplete and not fully functional — its homepage
        is devoid of content, the signup process does not work, and there is no visible navigation links into its content. By conducting a fresh,
        complete scrape of the underlying data and pairing it with a clean static architecture in Astro,
        this site brings the 2020 edition fully to life with all 20,189 sections, 26,529 critical apparatus
        footnotes, 7,938 linked cross-references, and all five research indices in a reliable, fast interface.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        <strong>An Authoritative Global Reference:</strong> Google Search often ranks <strong>tipitaka2500.github.io</strong> #1 for searches for the World Tipiṭaka Edition and therefore maintaining rigorous textual accuracy, deterministic verification, modern
        accessibility, and fast performance is a vital ongoing responsibility.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="index-card" style="padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      <h2 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--text-emphasis);">
        3. First Generation: Yuttadhammo Scrape &amp; Python Conversion
      </h2>
      <p>
        The initial version of <strong>tipitaka2500.github.io</strong> was built from Bhante
        Yuttadhammo’s Github repository <a href="https://github.com/yuttadhammo/World-Tipitaka" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">World-Tipitaka</a>, which preserved a scrape of the original Tipiṭaka website
        (2005 edition).
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        Using a series of custom Python conversion scripts, those archival files were cleaned,
        reorganized into a hierarchical folder structure matching the canonical divisions of the 40
        volumes, and wrapped in a static Bootstrap user interface with collapsible sidebar navigation
        and light/dark theming. While this made the Roman Pāḷi text fast and readable on the modern
        web, it lacked the critical apparatus footnotes, cross-references, and scholarly reference
        indices introduced in the updated 2020 edition.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="index-card" style="padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      <h2 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--text-emphasis);">
        4. Current Generation: Re-Scraped from Open Pāḷi &amp; Built with Astro (2020 Edition)
      </h2>
      <p>
        This updated version of the site is based on a fresh, complete scrape of the <a href="https://openpali.sajjhaya.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open Pāḷi</a> contents, bringing this website fully up to date with the <strong>2020 edition</strong> while adopting a modern static architecture powered by <a href="https://astro.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Astro</a>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
          <strong>100% Verified Canonical Content:</strong> The Pāḷi body text across all <strong>20,189 section pages</strong> and <strong>118,280 canonical paragraphs</strong> has been deterministically compared and verified against the previous repository to guarantee that the core canonical text is identical.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
          <strong>Clean Vanilla HTML Source:</strong> All 20,189 HTML files in <code>tipitaka/</code> are stored as clean, semantic, standards-compliant HTML5 without presentation framework bloat, while Astro provides the site layout, navigation, Rosely light/dark theme, and client-side interactivity.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
          <strong>Critical Apparatus Footnotes &amp; Linked Cross-References:</strong> Every page now includes its canonical footnotes (<strong>26,529 critical apparatus notes</strong> collating Sīhaḷa, Syāmaraṭṭha, Kambuja, Maramma, and PTS editions) with inline preview popovers and <strong>7,938 live cross-reference links</strong> that jump directly to the cited book and paragraph.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
          <strong>Complete 2020 Reference Indices &amp; Concordances:</strong> The site now includes interactive, searchable editions of the five reference databases from the 2020 edition.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0;">
          <strong>Dictionary Lookup &amp; Compound Deconstructor:</strong> Hover over (desktop) or tap (mobile) any Pāḷi word in the canonical text to view instant grammatical inflections rendered in the author's textbook notation symbols, English definitions from DPD, and compound/sandhi deconstruction into clickable component words.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="index-card" style="padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      <h2 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--text-emphasis);">
        5. Dictionary Lookup &amp; Textbook Grammar Notation
      </h2>
      <p>
        To make reading and studying the canonical Pāḷi text seamless for students, practitioners, and scholars
        alike, version 3.2 integrates an on-demand <strong>Dictionary Lookup</strong> and
        <strong>Compound/Sandhi Deconstructor</strong> directly into every reading page:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
          <strong>Digital Pāḷi Dictionary (DPD) Integration:</strong> Word definitions, roots, and
          morphological constructions are derived from the comprehensive <a href="https://digitalpalidictionary.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Digital Pāḷi Dictionary</a>.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
          <strong>Textbook Grammar Notation:</strong> Grammatical inflections are encoded using
          intuitive visual symbols from the author’s <a href="https://christham.net/buddhavacana/pali-textbook/0-status/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pāḷi Textbook</a> (e.g. <code>🚹①⨀</code> for masculine
          nominative singular, <code>▶️🤟🟢</code> for present 3rd person active verb), providing
          instant syntactic clarity at a glance without cryptic abbreviation clutter.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
          <strong>Compound &amp; Sandhi Deconstruction:</strong> Powered by the DPD deconstructor database,
          complex sandhi and compound forms (such as <em>evamme</em> → <em>evaṃ + me</em>, <em>tasseva</em> → <em>tassa + eva</em>, <em>hotīti</em> → <em>hoti + iti</em>)
          are automatically split into interactive constituent pills. Clicking any component word
          immediately retrieves its individual grammatical analysis and definition.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
          <strong>Client-Side Zero-Latency Sharding:</strong> The entire dictionary database of over
          <strong>1,178,000 Pāḷi word forms</strong> is split into 786 static JSON shards. The browser
          downloads only the relevant shard on demand using binary search and caches it locally,
          ensuring zero server latency, complete privacy, and full offline usability.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0;">
          <strong>User Controlled:</strong> Dictionary lookup can be easily toggled ON or OFF at any
          time using the <strong>📖 Dictionary Lookup</strong> button in the top header or mobile sidebar.
          For a complete guide to all grammar notation symbols, see the <a href="/guide.html#dict-lookup"><strong>Quick Guide →</strong></a>.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="index-card" style="padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      <h2 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--text-emphasis);">
        6. Research in Markdown, Offline Archive &amp; PWA
      </h2>
      <p>
        To support computational linguistics, digital humanities, and deep offline study anywhere:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
          <strong>Tipiṭaka in Markdown (with Corrections):</strong> While this website preserves 100% textual
          fidelity to the official World Tipiṭaka Edition database (retaining even idiosyncratic readings or
          slips in the original digital edition), a version incorporating verified textual corrections and
          collated errata is available for research and study in clean Markdown format at{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/tipitaka-md/tipitaka2500" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>tipitaka-md/tipitaka2500</strong></a>.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
          <strong>Offline Pāḷi Grammar Dictionaries (Multiple Formats):</strong> The inflection dictionary and
          textbook grammar notation system can be downloaded for offline use in <strong>Apple Dictionary</strong> (macOS/iOS),
          <strong>GoldenDict</strong>, and <strong>MDict</strong> formats from the releases page of the{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/ChristineTham/pali_grammar" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>pali_grammar</strong> repository</a>,
          or built from source with custom options.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
          <strong>Progressive Web App (PWA):</strong> This website is fully installable as an app on your phone,
          tablet, or computer. Using modern Service Workers, it automatically caches the core navigation shell,
          previously visited reading pages, and accessed dictionary entries for instant offline study.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0;">
          <strong>Full Offline Archive Download:</strong> For complete offline access to all 40 volumes, 20,189 sections,
          and dictionary shards without an internet connection, a pre-packaged offline zip archive (~354 MB)
          is available on the <a href="https://github.com/tipitaka2500/tipitaka2500.github.io/releases" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>GitHub Releases page</strong></a>.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

